WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.430
- This episode of LegalEagle

00:00:01.430 --> 00:00:03.120
was made possible by Skillshare.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.580
Learn to think like a lawyer
for free for two months

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.150
by clicking on the link
in the description.

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:11.300
Unidentified federal officers
wearing full camouflage,

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.260
jumping out of unmarked vehicles,

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.410
picking up seemingly random protestors,

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:17.750
handcuffing them, and then speeding away.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:20.030
This isn't George Orwell's "1984."

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:23.650
This is America, 2020, Portland, Oregon.

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:25.560
There have been over 50 straight days

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.050
of protests in Portland, Oregon,

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:29.060
sparked by the murders of George Floyd,

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.580
Breonna Taylor, and other
citizens killed by police.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.270
These protests have been mostly peaceful,

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:34.760
but a few people have committed

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:36.830
acts of vandalism and property damage.

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:39.030
But back in June,
President Trump activated

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:41.540
at least a dozen different
law enforcement agencies

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:45.320
to "restore order" during
protests in Washington, DC.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:48.750
- Thousands and thousands
of heavily-armed soldiers.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:50.250
- At the time, the federal government

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:51.520
justified this show of force

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:55.300
with vague references to
DC's status as not a state,

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.680
but the show of force
actually had little to do

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.560
with DC's precarious legal status.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.360
Instead, the response in
DC was more like a dry run,

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:05.910
a first attempt at using
federal police to stop protests.

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:08.270
Now, more than a hundred
law enforcement officers

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:10.310
have been deployed to Portland, Oregon,

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:11.620
and many of those officers

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.310
are not identifying themselves verbally

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:15.710
or through insignia on their uniforms.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:16.990
They're driving around in vehicles

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:18.640
not marked as law enforcement.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:20.510
And they refuse to say who they are,

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.630
even to those that they arrest.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:25.730
They have apparently kidnapped
protesters off the street

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:27.390
and taken them to jail cells.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.900
They've gone on the offensive
and they've shot protesters

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:31.670
with kinetic impact munitions.

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:34.450
Federal officers have also
sprayed tear gas at protesters,

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:37.110
prompting Oregon moms
to form a human shield

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:39.640
to protect people who were
peacefully protesting.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.890
They've attacked a Navy veteran
who came to ask officers

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:44.250
whether they understood the oath

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.280
that they took to the US Constitution,

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:50.740
who just didn't respond
like an absolute boss.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.610
All of this happened without the approval

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:53.960
of Oregon state officials.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:55.990
All in all, this is a
chilling show of force

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:57.920
in an allegedly free society.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:00.940
Are we witnessing military
detention of protestors?

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:03.080
Does the federal government
have authority to do this?

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:05.270
And do protesters have
the right to fight back

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:06.780
and stand their ground against people

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:09.340
who are not clearly
identified as law enforcement?

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:12.820
And most importantly, how
worried should we all be?

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:16.720
(reverent orchestral music)

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.960
Hey, LegalEagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.180
because there are reports of protestors

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.370
in Portland, Oregon being disappeared

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:26.060
by federal law enforcement
potentially operating

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:28.310
outside of its jurisdictional limits.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:29.920
Now, in this episode,
we're going to explain

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.870
what the heck is going on in Portland.

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:33.490
And if you're upset
about what's happening,

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:34.323
you're not alone.

00:02:34.323 --> 00:02:36.250
In fact, Oregon has already filed suit

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:38.840
against the federal government
to stop these tactics.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:40.160
But let's begin at the beginning

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.120
with the jurisdiction of
federal law enforcement officers

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.710
operating in the states.

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:47.130
The basic questions are which
law enforcement agencies

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:49.500
have authority over
federal land and property

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:52.670
and under what circumstances
can federal law enforcement

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:54.420
go beyond just federal land

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:57.420
and operate within the
jurisdiction of the state?

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.040
Now, Congress has the authority

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.790
to create law enforcement organizations

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:03.080
to patrol and protect
areas of federal property.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:05.830
Violations occurring
within the federal land

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:08.930
will then be handled by federal
law enforcement officers.

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:11.760
For example, the Bureau of Land
Management has the authority

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.490
to undertake traditional
police activities on BLM,

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:18.510
the other BLM, lands, which
include national parks,

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:21.070
but they cannot make
arrests under state laws

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:22.820
unless they have written authorization

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:24.720
by state or local officials.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.030
According to an opinion by
the Office of Legal Counsel

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:29.310
in 2012, federal enforcement personnel

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.660
lack the authority to make
arrests under state law.

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:32.970
And remember, state law is generally

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:33.930
the one that we think of

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:35.860
when we think of general
criminal activities,

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:39.000
the stuff that police
often arrest people under,

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:40.900
unless that authority is expressly granted

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:42.890
by either federal or state law

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:45.000
and the federal officers'
exercise of authority

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:46.930
complies with the Purpose Act,

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:49.220
which requires that federal funds be used

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.890
only for the purposes for
which they were appropriated.

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:54.070
By the way, I am deeply
indebted to law professor

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:56.150
and Lawfare contributor
Steve Vladeck on this.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:59.350
He's on Twitter at @steve_vladeck.

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:01.650
His research saved me a ton
of time putting this together

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:02.610
and you should really follow him

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:04.800
if you're into national security law.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.490
So, which law enforcement agencies

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:08.460
are active in situations like this?

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:10.610
Well, the law enforcement
response in Washington, DC

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:13.280
involved many different
agencies and the National Guard.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:14.150
And by most accounts,

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:17.200
the DC response was coordinated
by Attorney General Barr.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:18.560
Barr has legal authority

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.520
to coordinate law enforcement officers

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:23.020
who are under the jurisdiction
of the Department of Justice,

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:26.080
like the FBI and the
Federal Bureau of Prisons.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:28.920
In contrast, the federal
officers who are in Portland

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:31.770
appear to be from the Customs
and Border Protection.

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:33.380
CBP is under the jurisdiction

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:35.410
of the Department of Homeland Security.

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:38.410
So, let's talk about the
CBP's authority in Portland.

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:41.400
Does it have legal authority
to operate in Portland?

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.510
Well, yes, it probably does.

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:45.900
The Fourth Amendment of the
Constitution protects Americans

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:48.370
from random and arbitrary
stops and searches.

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:50.920
However, these basic
constitutional principles

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:54.040
do not apply fully at the "borders."

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:55.100
At border crossings,

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.230
including the ports of entry by the sea,

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:58.780
federal authorities do not need a warrant

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:01.720
or even suspicion of wrongdoing
to justify conducting

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:04.320
what the courts have
called a "routine search,"

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:06.540
such as searching luggage or a vehicle.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.150
The federal officers cannot
suspend the US Constitution,

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:11.080
but the Supreme Court has given them

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:13.070
more leeway in these activities.

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:15.870
Federal regulations approved
by the Supreme Court,

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:17.780
give the CBP authority to operate

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:21.520
within 100 miles of any
US "external boundary."

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:23.700
The closest international
border to Portland

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:25.660
is the Pacific Ocean, and the ocean

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:27.520
is less than a hundred
miles away from Portland,

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:30.300
which gives the CBP
specific enforcement powers.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.130
This relaxation of
constitutional protections

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:35.630
within a hundred miles
of any external border

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:38.670
and/or an ocean has
received a lotta criticism

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:41.150
from civil libertarians,
which correctly point out

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:43.030
that the vast majority of Americans

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:46.330
live within a hundred miles
of an external border.

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:48.100
Although CBP has legal authority

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:50.450
to enforce federal laws in Portland,

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:52.430
they cannot enforce state laws.

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:54.870
For example, CBP must
have reasonable suspicion

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:57.240
of an immigration violation
or violation of federal law

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:00.080
in order to pull someone
over and to stop them.

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:01.450
But, of course, not surprisingly,

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:03.460
the federal government does have authority

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:07.420
to protect federal property
and to investigate offenses

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:10.230
against that federal
property and federal land.

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:11.930
And generally, the federal agency

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:14.300
that's tasked with
protecting federal property

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:17.810
is the Federal Protective
Service, not the CBP.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:20.040
But here, and perhaps in the future,

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:21.980
Customs and Border Patrol
is being authorized

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:24.190
under 40 USC 1315,

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:26.910
which allows the Department
of Homeland Security secretary

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:29.640
to designate other DHS officers

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:31.810
to backstop the Federal
Protective Service.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:34.670
Section 1315, which was
enacted over a decade ago,

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:36.450
empowers the Department
of Homeland Security

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:38.217
to "make arrests without a warrant

00:06:38.217 --> 00:06:39.817
"for any offense against the United States

00:06:39.817 --> 00:06:42.587
"committed in the presence
of the officer or agent

00:06:42.587 --> 00:06:45.687
"or for any felony
cognizable under the laws

00:06:45.687 --> 00:06:48.047
"of the United States
if the officer or agent

00:06:48.047 --> 00:06:49.807
"has reasonable grounds to believe

00:06:49.807 --> 00:06:52.407
"that the person to be
arrested has committed

00:06:52.407 --> 00:06:54.320
"or is committing a felony."

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:57.020
And under this section,
unless the federal officer

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:59.850
personally witnesses a
federal crime being committed

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:01.810
or has a reason to believe

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:04.050
an individual committed a federal offense,

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.710
the only authority under Section 1315

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:10.320
is to investigate offenses
against federal property,

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:12.280
which, again, is generally the purview

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:13.970
of the Federal Protective Services.

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:16.820
And, really here, the $64,000 question

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:19.357
is, "What qualifies as an investigation

00:07:19.357 --> 00:07:21.510
"of an offense against federal property?"

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:24.000
But regardless of the
authorization of these regulations,

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.000
they're still limited by the Constitution.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:28.260
In order to be able to
physically stop someone,

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:29.660
you need reasonable suspicion.

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:31.050
And according to the Supreme Court,

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:34.100
in order to put someone in
a van and drive them away,

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:35.910
you have to have probable cause.

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:38.950
And here, there are reports,
admittedly one-sided reports

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:40.910
coming from the protesters themselves,

00:07:40.910 --> 00:07:43.070
that it doesn't seem
like the federal officers

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:44.990
have those kind of justifications.

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:46.670
And it also appears that
the federal officers

00:07:46.670 --> 00:07:50.100
are operating pretty far away
from the federal property

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:51.620
that they've been tasked to protect

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:54.700
and potentially to
investigate crimes related to.

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:57.820
- [Ken] Well, we're 49 straight days

00:07:57.820 --> 00:08:00.600
into violence and destruction in Portland,

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:03.350
so it justifies the response

00:08:03.350 --> 00:08:05.800
because violence and destruction continue.

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:08.170
- Now, that's not to say
that the protests in Portland

00:08:08.170 --> 00:08:09.710
have been entirely nonviolent.

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:12.510
It can be the case, as
it appears to be here,

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:15.470
that there can be some
people who are out rioting

00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:17.930
and committing acts of
violence and vandalism

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:21.340
while the vast majority of
people are peacefully protesting.

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:22.263
And the thing about that

00:08:22.263 --> 00:08:25.270
is that even if simple acts of vandalism

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:27.160
did justify the use of force,

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:29.000
and query whether that's true or not,

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:30.700
that doesn't justify using force

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:33.960
against the overwhelmingly
peaceful protestors of Portland

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:35.550
and lots of other American cities.

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:38.340
- [Ken] This is a posture
we intend to continue,

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:41.600
not just in Portland, but
in any of the facilities

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:43.610
that we're responsible
for around the country.

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:46.200
- America's not under siege by protestors.

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:48.020
And there's a concept
in the First Amendment

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:49.420
called the heckler's veto,

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:53.240
that you are not allowed
to use the violent reaction

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:57.370
of a minority to quell the
speech activity of the majority.

00:08:57.370 --> 00:09:00.220
That's a heckler's veto,
and we don't want that.

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:01.560
But you don't have to take my word for it.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:04.430
Let's kick it over to our
correspondent in the field,

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:08.030
a Portland resident and
a mom in the mom wall,

00:09:08.030 --> 00:09:11.610
and my sister, Lil' Leg'l Eag'l.

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:13.290
- [Sister] LegalEagle,
we are here on a weekday.

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:15.310
It's about 9:45 p.m.
(protestors clapping)

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:17.490
And as you can hear,
we're singing dorky songs

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:20.322
and the moms and dads
are out in the thousands.

00:09:20.322 --> 00:09:21.155
(crowd cheering)

00:09:21.155 --> 00:09:23.590
It feels very safe actually right now.

00:09:23.590 --> 00:09:25.500
So, even though everybody
has come prepared

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:30.500
with tear gas accoutrements,
some goggles, some helmets,

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:32.160
people are passing out water,

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:34.640
so far it has felt very peaceful

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:37.580
and everybody is singing
and getting along very well,

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:40.080
so it's been a really
wonderful experience so far.

00:09:40.931 --> 00:09:44.260
♪ Hands up, please don't shoot me ♪

00:09:44.260 --> 00:09:45.770
- The crowd has so far been very peaceful.

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:48.000
So, how this all started is a protest

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:48.833
for Black Lives Matter,

00:09:48.833 --> 00:09:51.340
of course, spun off by the
murder of George Floyd.

00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:53.940
And since then, here in
the past three nights,

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:55.590
there have been thousands of mom

00:09:55.590 --> 00:09:58.330
that have shown up to
create a wall of moms.

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:00.680
So, the idea here is that
all of us are in yellow.

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:02.980
The dads are out tonight
as well in orange.

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:06.450
And the theme is that
we're protecting the youth

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:09.910
that are indiscriminately
and illegally being abducted,

00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:11.990
beaten, any number of things by the feds

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:13.120
that have come into our city.

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:16.040
So, so far the night has
been absolutely lovely.

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:18.460
It's a warm, balmy evening.

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:19.870
People have been passing out
(toy squeaking)

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:21.460
these little pig squeakers,

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:22.880
which has been kind of delightful,

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:24.380
very Portland of the city.

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:25.930
And otherwise, we've been singing.

00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:29.410
There have been people
of color representing

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:32.330
and speaking up on stage
and a lot of chants,

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:34.250
so it's been a really nice night.

00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:35.083
- Thanks, Little Sis.

00:10:35.083 --> 00:10:36.440
Really truly terrifying stuff.

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:37.810
I haven't seen that kind of carnage

00:10:37.810 --> 00:10:40.140
since the Women's March of '17.

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:42.060
Stay safe out there and
don't get a paper cut

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:43.420
on all those signs.

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:44.690
- [All] Feds stay clear!

00:10:44.690 --> 00:10:45.883
The moms are here!

00:10:45.883 --> 00:10:48.750
- So, the next question,
naturally, is what federal laws

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:52.010
are CBP officers trying
to enforce in Portland?

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:53.170
Unlike Washington, DC,

00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:55.250
where there's quite a
lot of federal property,

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:56.930
Oregon really doesn't
have that much property

00:10:56.930 --> 00:10:58.380
that's under the exclusive jurisdiction

00:10:58.380 --> 00:10:59.740
of the federal government.

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:02.170
Federal law enforcement
officers can arrest someone

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:04.510
who vandalizes a federal
building like a courthouse,

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:06.830
which there are some in downtown Portland,

00:11:06.830 --> 00:11:09.030
and federal immigration
authorities can arrest people

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:11.110
for suspicion of
violating immigration laws

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:12.960
and for violating any federal law,

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:14.600
provided the federal offense happened

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:15.950
in the officer's presence.

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:17.550
But federal law enforcement officers

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:20.130
don't have the authority
to enforce state laws

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:21.870
on non-federal property.

00:11:21.870 --> 00:11:23.690
And when you're talking about vandalism

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:24.980
of non-federal property,

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:27.250
that would clearly be a state-based law.

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:29.130
And here, the governor of Oregon

00:11:29.130 --> 00:11:31.710
does not want federal law
enforcement in her state.

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:33.650
That leaves us with a
complicated situation

00:11:33.650 --> 00:11:37.070
that's susceptible to abuse by
overzealous federal officers.

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:39.340
So, are federal officers simply protecting

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:41.910
federal courthouses from
vandalism in Portland?

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:43.530
It definitely doesn't look that way.

00:11:43.530 --> 00:11:46.810
Reports suggest that CBP
officers are roaming the streets

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:48.760
a considerable distance
from federal property.

00:11:48.760 --> 00:11:50.710
For example, protestor Mark Pettibone

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:53.220
was suddenly seized by camo-clad people

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:55.680
and thrown into an unmarked minivan.

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:58.450
Pettibone was taken to a
cell in a federal courthouse

00:11:58.450 --> 00:12:00.530
where he was asked if he
would waive his rights.

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:03.350
He answered, "No," and was
then apparently released.

00:12:03.350 --> 00:12:05.280
Pettibone wasn't charged with a crime.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:06.730
He says that the men who grabbed him

00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:08.490
did not identify themselves.

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:11.110
CBP said that Pettibone and
others who were detained

00:12:11.110 --> 00:12:13.130
were wanted for destruction
of federal property

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:14.910
or assault on federal officers,

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:17.360
but there's been no proof of
these allegations yet though.

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:19.510
And the fact that these
people are being detained,

00:12:19.510 --> 00:12:21.770
thrown in a cell, and then
released without charges,

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:23.880
makes these arrests seem pretty dubious.

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:26.640
CBP denies that its
officers are not identified.

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:27.770
The agency told reporters

00:12:27.770 --> 00:12:30.120
that their uniforms have a CBP patch,

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:32.340
but officers are not identified by name

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:33.590
because they might be doxxed,

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:35.570
which might put their lives in danger.

00:12:35.570 --> 00:12:37.670
This might strike some of
you as an acceptable reason

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:39.660
not to identify officers by name,

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:43.110
but note that even Army
soldiers in Iraq and Afghanistan

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:46.310
wear names on their uniforms,
both in English and Arabic.

00:12:46.310 --> 00:12:47.960
Is the average Portland protestor

00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:50.540
a greater menace than ISIS or Al-Qaeda?

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:52.300
And by the way, if you
want a great breakdown

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:53.270
between the differences

00:12:53.270 --> 00:12:55.330
between police tactics and Army tactics,

00:12:55.330 --> 00:12:57.190
check out my friend
Knowing Better's channel,

00:12:57.190 --> 00:12:58.820
where he talks about his experience

00:12:58.820 --> 00:13:00.570
serving in the military in Afghanistan

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:02.960
and the differences
between military and police

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:05.520
and the overlap between the two as well.

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:07.947
Legal commentator Ken
White probably put it best:

00:13:07.947 --> 00:13:10.407
"Federalism, except for occupying cities

00:13:10.407 --> 00:13:11.637
"and beating and detaining people

00:13:11.637 --> 00:13:13.340
"on suspicion of vandalism."

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:14.950
So, now we get to the heart of the matter.

00:13:14.950 --> 00:13:17.380
Did federal LEOs violate the law?

00:13:17.380 --> 00:13:19.990
Well, let's talk about the
lack of identification.

00:13:19.990 --> 00:13:21.620
Why is it bad for law enforcement

00:13:21.620 --> 00:13:23.050
to go around without identification?

00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:25.340
Well, first and foremost,
a person or suspect

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:27.370
might refuse to respond to an order

00:13:27.370 --> 00:13:28.730
from a law enforcement official

00:13:28.730 --> 00:13:30.900
who doesn't clearly identify themselves.

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:31.733
There's also the danger

00:13:31.733 --> 00:13:33.170
that various law enforcement officers

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:34.810
might not be able to recognize each other,

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:36.760
especially since many different military

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:40.310
and police groups are involved
in both DC and Portland.

00:13:40.310 --> 00:13:42.940
And riot helmets often
have identifying numbers

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:45.020
on their backs, partly for this reason.

00:13:45.020 --> 00:13:46.410
But the big reason, obviously,

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:48.450
is that if officers don't
identify themselves,

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:51.330
then they can't be held
accountable for their misbehavior.

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:53.600
This is especially egregious
because the protests

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:56.360
are directly calling for
police accountability.

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:58.610
Anonymity might be a good thing in war,

00:13:58.610 --> 00:14:00.830
but it's very bad when the officers

00:14:00.830 --> 00:14:03.790
are controlling demonstrations
in their own country.

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:06.290
And the precise legal question
of whether it's reasonable

00:14:06.290 --> 00:14:09.280
for an officer to refuse to
identify him or herself by name

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:11.710
and state who he works for
has not really been decided

00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:12.930
by federal courts.

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:14.740
Obviously, when officers are undercover,

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:17.440
it would be reasonable for
them to remain anonymous.

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:19.550
But in situations like a protest,

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:21.840
it's vitally important for people to know

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:23.580
that they're being stopped by police

00:14:23.580 --> 00:14:25.190
instead of by civilians.

00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:27.140
And courts have generally
held that officers

00:14:27.140 --> 00:14:29.570
should identify themselves
in these situations,

00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:31.560
but it's still a novel legal issue,

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:33.270
at least from a federal standpoint.

00:14:33.270 --> 00:14:35.330
There's no federal law that requires

00:14:35.330 --> 00:14:38.560
federal law enforcement
officers to wear a name tag,

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:42.060
to wear a specific identifiable number

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:44.360
that can be attributed to
the individual officer,

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:47.570
in part probably because
federal law enforcement

00:14:47.570 --> 00:14:49.430
has been very, very curtailed

00:14:49.430 --> 00:14:51.200
in where it can be used in the US

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:52.980
and it's really very recently

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:57.050
that they are expanding
outside of their clear mandate.

00:14:57.050 --> 00:14:59.050
But just because there's no
federal law that applies here

00:14:59.050 --> 00:15:01.090
doesn't mean that state
law doesn't control.

00:15:01.090 --> 00:15:04.010
And while there is no federal
law requiring federal officers

00:15:04.010 --> 00:15:06.410
to wear insignia identifying themselves

00:15:06.410 --> 00:15:08.070
or individual name tags,

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:10.790
Oregon law does require any
law enforcement officers

00:15:10.790 --> 00:15:12.880
purporting to enforce state law

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:15.950
to conspicuously display an
official identification card

00:15:15.950 --> 00:15:18.540
showing the officer's lawful authority.

00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:21.760
Oregon authorizes federal
officers to enforce state law,

00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:24.130
but only under specific circumstances.

00:15:24.130 --> 00:15:25.950
A federal officer may arrest someone

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:27.980
for a crime committed in
the officer's presence

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:29.880
if the person has probable cause.

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:31.670
Under Oregon's authorization

00:15:31.670 --> 00:15:33.820
of federal law enforcement officers,

00:15:33.820 --> 00:15:35.310
a federal officer may arrest someone

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:37.290
for a crime committed in
the officer's presence,

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:39.000
if the person has probable cause,

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:41.510
the officer informs the
person to be arrested

00:15:41.510 --> 00:15:44.210
of the officer's authority
and reason for the arrest,

00:15:44.210 --> 00:15:46.630
and that the officer
takes the arrested person

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:49.630
to a peace officer or
magistrate without delay.

00:15:49.630 --> 00:15:51.320
And there's one other legal requirement.

00:15:51.320 --> 00:15:53.100
The federal officer cannot make arrests

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:54.750
for state criminal violations

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:56.560
unless they receive Oregon training

00:15:56.560 --> 00:15:58.290
and are certified by the state.

00:15:58.290 --> 00:15:59.970
This tells us some important things.

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:02.700
First, a federal CBP
officer could arrest someone

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:04.780
for a violation of Oregon state law,

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:07.000
but only if they have probable cause.

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:08.550
Second, the arrest is only legal

00:16:08.550 --> 00:16:09.910
if the officer tells the person

00:16:09.910 --> 00:16:11.650
the basis for their authority,

00:16:11.650 --> 00:16:13.460
who they are and who they work for.

00:16:13.460 --> 00:16:14.900
And third, the federal officer

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:17.710
must swiftly bring that person
before a judge or magistrate

00:16:17.710 --> 00:16:20.290
so they're notified about
their charges and rights.

00:16:20.290 --> 00:16:22.760
And fourth, the federal
officer must be certified

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:25.750
by the state of Oregon to
make arrests pursuant to law,

00:16:25.750 --> 00:16:28.910
which takes us to the first
of many lawsuits here.

00:16:28.910 --> 00:16:29.890
Based on what we know,

00:16:29.890 --> 00:16:31.890
it doesn't seem like the
federal officers in Portland

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:35.110
can prove that they meet one
or any of these conditions.

00:16:35.110 --> 00:16:38.140
Oregon Governor Kate Brown
and Portland Mayor Ted Wheeler

00:16:38.140 --> 00:16:40.880
have implored DHS to remove CBP

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:43.150
and other federal officers from the state.

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:45.210
The Oregon Department of
Justice has filed a lawsuit

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:47.210
accusing DHS and others agencies

00:16:47.210 --> 00:16:48.990
of unlawful law enforcement.

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:51.450
In addition to CBP, the
state has identified

00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:53.370
other active law enforcement agencies

00:16:53.370 --> 00:16:55.030
as the US Marshals Service

00:16:55.030 --> 00:16:56.610
and the Federal Protective Service.

00:16:56.610 --> 00:16:58.930
The failure of federal
officers to identify themselves

00:16:58.930 --> 00:17:00.950
is actually one of the
reasons for this lawsuit.

00:17:00.950 --> 00:17:03.570
The complaint names 10
John Doe defendants,

00:17:03.570 --> 00:17:05.200
who are just placeholders

00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:07.460
that you put in a lawsuit complaint

00:17:07.460 --> 00:17:09.990
until you know the actual
identity of that person,

00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:12.787
and names the 10 Doe defendants who,

00:17:12.787 --> 00:17:13.877
"have made it impossible

00:17:13.877 --> 00:17:15.847
"for them to be individually identified

00:17:15.847 --> 00:17:17.887
"by carrying out law enforcement actions

00:17:17.887 --> 00:17:20.267
"without wearing any
identifying information,

00:17:20.267 --> 00:17:23.340
"even so much as the
agency that employs them."

00:17:23.340 --> 00:17:25.510
The lawsuit seeks a
temporary restraining order

00:17:25.510 --> 00:17:28.070
to prevent federal authorities
from unlawfully detaining

00:17:28.070 --> 00:17:29.990
Oregon residents like Mark Pettibone.

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:32.257
According to the suit,
"Oregonians have the right

00:17:32.257 --> 00:17:34.027
"to walk through downtown
Portland at night

00:17:34.027 --> 00:17:35.967
"and in the early hours of the morning.

00:17:35.967 --> 00:17:37.847
"Ordinarily, a person exercising his right

00:17:37.847 --> 00:17:39.207
"to walk through the streets of Portland

00:17:39.207 --> 00:17:41.397
"who is confronted by anonymous men

00:17:41.397 --> 00:17:44.937
"in military-type fatigues and
ordered into an unmarked van

00:17:44.937 --> 00:17:47.027
"can reasonably assume
that he's being kidnapped

00:17:47.027 --> 00:17:48.600
"and is the victim of a crime."

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:50.330
This suit alleges that this is

00:17:50.330 --> 00:17:51.970
an unreasonable search and seizure

00:17:51.970 --> 00:17:53.780
in violation of the Fourth Amendment

00:17:53.780 --> 00:17:55.810
which will be repeated
if the federal government

00:17:55.810 --> 00:17:57.790
is not enjoined from its activities.

00:17:57.790 --> 00:17:59.750
The complaint alleges that this activity

00:17:59.750 --> 00:18:01.470
of federal law enforcement officers

00:18:01.470 --> 00:18:04.230
violate Oregon citizens' First
Amendment right to protest,

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:06.377
since, "Citizens who are reasonably afraid

00:18:06.377 --> 00:18:08.857
"of being picked up and
shoved into unmarked vans,

00:18:08.857 --> 00:18:10.717
"possibly by federal officers,

00:18:10.717 --> 00:18:13.357
"possibly by individuals
opposed to protests,

00:18:13.357 --> 00:18:15.817
"will feel compelled to stay away

00:18:15.817 --> 00:18:17.317
"for their own personal safety

00:18:17.317 --> 00:18:19.977
"and will therefore be
unable to express themselves

00:18:19.977 --> 00:18:22.290
"in the way that they have a right to do."

00:18:22.290 --> 00:18:23.700
The state attorney general alleges

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:25.490
that the federal government did not notify

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:27.520
or coordinate with state officers.

00:18:27.520 --> 00:18:29.640
Essentially, the state is saying
that the federal government

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:31.000
just showed up to the protests

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:33.440
without any requests from
state or local authorities

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:35.120
or training by the state.

00:18:35.120 --> 00:18:37.400
The ACLU of Oregon has
also filed a lawsuit

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:39.060
against the Department
of Homeland Security

00:18:39.060 --> 00:18:40.550
and the US Marshals Service.

00:18:40.550 --> 00:18:43.440
The ACLU's lawsuit seeks to
block federal law enforcement

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:45.920
from dispersing, arresting,
threatening to arrest,

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:47.970
or using physical force
against journalists

00:18:47.970 --> 00:18:49.360
or legal observers.

00:18:49.360 --> 00:18:50.320
The plaintiffs in that case

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:52.460
include the newspaper the Portland Mercury

00:18:52.460 --> 00:18:54.580
and individual journalists
and legal observers

00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:57.560
who say that they were attacked
by federal law enforcement.

00:18:57.560 --> 00:18:59.640
The ACLU is planning several lawsuits

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:00.590
on behalf of protesters

00:19:00.590 --> 00:19:02.450
who say that their rights were violated,

00:19:02.450 --> 00:19:04.230
so this may be the tip of the iceberg.

00:19:04.230 --> 00:19:07.110
So, is this a constitutional crisis

00:19:07.110 --> 00:19:09.710
of the federal government
against the states?

00:19:09.710 --> 00:19:12.750
Well, Governor Brown has told
federal officers to get out

00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:14.660
and President Trump said no.

00:19:14.660 --> 00:19:16.460
The federal government
has officially refused

00:19:16.460 --> 00:19:18.580
the state's request to withdraw
federal law enforcement

00:19:18.580 --> 00:19:19.770
from the state.

00:19:19.770 --> 00:19:21.770
The state can argue that
it has certain powers

00:19:21.770 --> 00:19:23.660
reserved to it by the 10th Amendment.

00:19:23.660 --> 00:19:25.870
And while there's a real difficulty

00:19:25.870 --> 00:19:28.780
balancing governmental
response to domestic crises,

00:19:28.780 --> 00:19:31.050
the states generally have
flexibility and authority

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:34.190
to respond as they see the
needs of their citizens.

00:19:34.190 --> 00:19:35.023
And at the same time,

00:19:35.023 --> 00:19:36.390
the federal government also has a right

00:19:36.390 --> 00:19:38.670
to respond to federal crises.

00:19:38.670 --> 00:19:41.180
The video that I did a few
weeks back on posse comitatus

00:19:41.180 --> 00:19:44.330
and the Insurrection Act laid
out some of these issues.

00:19:44.330 --> 00:19:46.710
The founders were afraid of
concentrating too much power

00:19:46.710 --> 00:19:48.630
in the presidency or
the federal government.

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:50.890
And one of the checks on
the tyrannical government

00:19:50.890 --> 00:19:52.220
was to give states the power

00:19:52.220 --> 00:19:55.530
to overrule the federal government
during certain conflicts.

00:19:55.530 --> 00:19:56.760
And a lot of the power

00:19:56.760 --> 00:19:58.670
that we assume the federal government has

00:19:58.670 --> 00:20:00.680
might have been reserved for states

00:20:00.680 --> 00:20:02.450
because of the 10th Amendment.

00:20:02.450 --> 00:20:04.510
However, the Trump
Administration can counter

00:20:04.510 --> 00:20:07.040
and say that it's merely
protecting federal property

00:20:07.040 --> 00:20:08.960
and that the CBP is legally permitted

00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:10.410
to help Federal Protective Service

00:20:10.410 --> 00:20:11.940
protect federal buildings.

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:14.610
And that might effectively
give the president

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:15.690
a big personal army

00:20:15.690 --> 00:20:18.190
of more than 60,000
employees and officers.

00:20:18.190 --> 00:20:21.150
And while in the abstract this
might not be the worst thing,

00:20:21.150 --> 00:20:22.420
of course the federal government

00:20:22.420 --> 00:20:25.660
needs law enforcement officers
to enforce federal laws,

00:20:25.660 --> 00:20:28.010
unfortunately, we're
seeing a pattern here.

00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:29.440
As we've learned over
the past three months,

00:20:29.440 --> 00:20:30.810
the Trump Administration will meet

00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:32.210
vandalism and property damage

00:20:32.210 --> 00:20:35.170
with an overwhelming show
of paramilitary force

00:20:35.170 --> 00:20:36.780
against American citizens.

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:39.590
And in DC, effectively,

00:20:39.590 --> 00:20:42.010
Bill Barr, attorney
general, was in charge,

00:20:42.010 --> 00:20:44.160
but here it's the acting director

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:46.540
of Department of Homeland
Security Chad Wolf,

00:20:46.540 --> 00:20:48.520
who has been confirmed by the Senate.

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:51.400
I don't need invitations by the state,

00:20:51.400 --> 00:20:54.550
state mayors or state
governors to do our job.

00:20:54.550 --> 00:20:57.670
We're gonna do that whether
they like us there or not.

00:20:57.670 --> 00:21:00.240
- And his acting deputy, Ken Cuccinelli,

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:02.130
who's also the acting director

00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:04.820
of Citizenship and
Immigration Services, CIS,

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:07.490
has also not been confirmed by the Senate.

00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:09.230
And a federal court ruled that Cuccinelli

00:21:09.230 --> 00:21:11.180
was not lawfully
appointed to his position,

00:21:11.180 --> 00:21:12.290
and therefore he lacked authority

00:21:12.290 --> 00:21:14.940
to issue several directives
